 /*
- * Galaxy S9: Samsung S6SY761 (sec_ts) at 0x48 on GENI SE11 (0xa8c000),
- * GPIO31/32 qup11, polled. Its rails (S2DOS05 LDO2/LDO3) are boot-on, so
- * there is no power or reset sequence and no RPMh vote here. Protocol from
- * drivers/input/touchscreen/sec_ts of the Samsung/LineageOS kernel.
- * The I2C engine below is ios-sweet's, which follows:
- * SWEET SE7 FIFO I2C / GT9896 Yellowstone / Focaltech 54:52 bring-up.
- * Call once after the scheduler is running; all work runs on one thread.
- * Requires ABL-loaded GENI I2C firmware; votes L11C through sweet_rpmh.c.
- * No firmware/config upload or direct PMIC writes. Only IC data write:
- * zero to GT9896's event status after reading a report. See touch2/report.md.
+ * Galaxy S9: Samsung S6SY761 (sec_ts) at 0x48, read while its interrupt line
+ * (GPIO120, active low) is down. Protocol from drivers/input/touchscreen/sec_ts
+ * of the Samsung/LineageOS kernel.
+ *   - Power: the IC's rails are S2DOS05 LDO2 (dvdd 1.8 V) and LDO3 (avdd
+ *     3.0 V). The DT marks them regulator-boot-on, which makes Linux's regulator
+ *     core enable them; ABL leaves them off (EN 0x19, vsel already 0x8c). The
+ *     PMIC answers at 0x60 on a bit-banged bus (i2c-gpio: GPIO127/128). The only
+ *     PMIC writes: those two LDO enable bits, and their vsel if not 1.8/3.0 V.
+ *   - Bus: SE11 (0xa8c000) runs its I2C firmware GSI-only, and GSI DMA needs the
+ *     apps SMMU, whose registers do not answer without the bus votes Linux
+ *     makes. The touch's own pins, GPIO31/32, are bit-banged instead, the way
+ *     i2c-gpio does it.
+ * Proven first by s9port shim/m5.c (M5.4): power, boot status, id and five
+ * fingers at once. Only IC writes: CLEAR_EVENT_STACK, SET_TOUCHFUNCTION and
+ * SENSE_ON. Call once after the scheduler is running; all work runs on one
+ * thread.
  */
